\label{thm:main}
For every four finite discrete random variables,
\begin{equation}
64\operatorname{Ing}+10H(ABCD)\ge0.
\label{eq:theorem}
\end{equation}
Consequently, the supremum in~\eqref{eq:score} satisfies $M\le5/32$.

The following identity holds for every vector of the fifteen entropy coordinates:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
64\operatorname{Ing}+10h_N
={}&\sum_{g\in G}(2g\mathcal D+g\mathcal R)\\
&+10\sum_{X\in N}H(X\mid N\setminus\{X\})\\
&+30I(A;B\mid CD)+10I(C;D)\\
&+2I(C;D\mid A)+2I(C;D\mid B).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p532}
\end{equation}
To verify it, expand the conditional mutual informations into entropy coordinates and substitute~\eqref{eq:D} and~\eqref{eq:R}. The coefficient of $h_N$ is 10; the remaining coefficients are 64 times those in~\eqref{eq:entropy-form}. The ancillary verifier checks each of the fifteen coefficients exactly.

Every $g\mathcal D$ and $g\mathcal R$ is nonnegative by the corresponding published inequality with its variables renamed. The conditional entropies in the second line of~\eqref{eq:p532} and all mutual informations in its last two lines are nonnegative Shannon terms. Every scalar coefficient on the right is positive. This proves~\eqref{eq:theorem}. If $h_N>0$, division by $64h_N$ gives $-\operatorname{Ing}/h_N\le10/64=5/32$.

All four renamed expressions are evaluated on the same arbitrary joint distribution. No invariance of that distribution under $G$ is required. If $h_N=0$, all four variables are constant and~\eqref{eq:theorem} holds with equality.

Consider the rational vector $h^*$ defined by
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c|c}
S & h^*_S\\\hline
A,B & 9/16\\
C,D & 1/2\\
AB & 27/32\\
AC,AD,BC,BD & 25/32\\
CD,ABC,ABD,ACD,BCD,ABCD & 1
\end{array}
\label{eq:primal}
\end{equation}
It has $h_N^*=1$ and $\operatorname{Ing}(h^*)=-5/32$. Direct rational evaluation shows that it satisfies every Shannon row and every permutation of every inequality in all three catalogues. This is a finite check of 21,940 rows when the three catalogues are combined; the ancillary files contain the complete coefficient data and verifier. Thus each of these polyhedra has objective value at least $5/32$.

For the 2013 catalogue, identity~\eqref{eq:p532} supplies the matching upper bound because both $\mathcal D$ and $\mathcal R$ occur in that list. For DFZ combined with the 2016 list, $\mathcal D$ occurs in DFZ and $\mathcal R$ occurs in the 2016 list. The same identity applies to their union and to the stated smaller system. Hence all these optima are exactly $5/32$.

\label{thm:new}
For every four finite discrete random variables,
\begin{equation}
124\operatorname{Ing}+19H(ABCD)\ge0.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the supremum in~\eqref{eq:score} satisfies $M\le19/124$.

Let $\Pi$ be the polyhedron formed, as in Section~\ref{sec:full}, by the blocks of the four $G$-images of $\mathcal P$, which share the fifteen original coordinates; it has $4047$ coordinates. The entropy vector of $A,B,C,D$ extends to a point of $\Pi$, namely to the entropy vectors of the four joint distributions constructed by the copy steps. The ancillary file \texttt{new\_program\_19\_124.json} gives rational multipliers $\lambda_i\ge0$ on $1{,}862$ elemental Shannon rows $s_i$ and $\mu_j$ on $380$ copy equalities $e_j$ of these blocks such that
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Ing}=-\frac{19}{124}\,h_N+\sum_i\lambda_i s_i+\sum_j\mu_j e_j
\label{eq:newdual}
\end{equation}
holds identically in all $4047$ coordinates. Evaluating~\eqref{eq:newdual} at an extension of the entropy vector gives $\operatorname{Ing}\ge-\frac{19}{124}h_N$.
